format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

objective u1-isotope-not-radioactive
statement: Know that an isotope is not automatically radioactive: most of the atoms around us are stable isotopes.
misconception: Isotope means radioactive or man-made.

item u1-l4-tom-all-radioactive
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

statement:
    All isotopes are radioactive.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Isotope just means a version of an element. Most of the atoms around you are stable isotopes. Only some isotopes are radioactive.

item u1-l4-tom-c13-stable
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

statement:
    Carbon-13 is an isotope of carbon, and it is not radioactive.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Carbon-13 is a stable isotope. Being an isotope does not make an atom radioactive.

item u1-x-isotope-radioactive
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

prompt:
    Which statement about isotopes is true?

options:
    {"id":"some","label":"Some are stable and some are radioactive"}
    {"id":"all","label":"All of them are radioactive","note":"Most of the atoms around you are stable isotopes."}
    {"id":"none","label":"None of them are radioactive","note":"Some isotopes, such as carbon-14, are radioactive."}

answer: some

explanation:
    Isotopes can be stable or radioactive. Carbon-12 is stable and carbon-14 is radioactive.

item u1-x-isotope-stable-true
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-isotopes

statement:
    Carbon-12 is an isotope, and it is stable.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Carbon-12 is a stable isotope of carbon.
